Thanks — I can implement this. First, a short design + choices, then a ready-to-run implementation (Go) and a systemd unit to run it as a daemon.

Design summary (why this is safe and robust)
- Use an OS-level watcher (fsnotify on Linux/macOS) to get events efficiently.
- Serialize all log writes through a dedicated logger goroutine so logging never races with watchers or signal handling.
- Handle OS signals via the runtime (os/signal.Notify in Go) instead of doing heavy work inside a raw C signal handler. That preserves full logging capability because signals are delivered to a Go channel and handled in normal Go code (not inside an async-signal context).
- On termination signals (SIGINT/SIGTERM) we log the shutdown event, flush and fsync the log file, then exit cleanly.
- On SIGHUP we reopen the log file (useful for logrotate).
- Each log write is appended and fsynced so the events.log remains durable even across crashes (configurable if you want higher throughput).
- If you specifically must use C-level signal handlers (raw handlers), we should use the self-pipe trick or signalfd to avoid calling non-async-signal-safe functions in handlers. The Go approach avoids that complexity while meeting the requirement to "maintain full logging capability at all times."

Go implementation
- Requires Go (1.18+).
- Uses fsnotify (github.com/fsnotify/fsnotify).

Save as main.go:

```go
package main

import (
	"flag"
	"fmt"
	"log"
	"os"
	"os/signal"
	"path/filepath"
	"sync"
	"syscall"
	"time"

	"github.com/fsnotify/fsnotify"
)

type logMessage struct {
	timestamp time.Time
	text      string
}

func loggerLoop(path string, msgs <-chan logMessage, reopen <-chan struct{}, done chan<- struct{}) {
	// Ensure log directory exists
	if err := os.MkdirAll(filepath.Dir(path), 0o755); err != nil {
		log.Fatalf("mkdir log dir: %v", err)
	}

	openFile := func() *os.File {
		f, err := os.OpenFile(path, os.O_CREATE|os.O_WRONLY|os.O_APPEND, 0o644)
		if err != nil {
			log.Fatalf("open log file: %v", err)
		}
		return f
	}

	f := openFile()
	defer func() {
		f.Sync()
		f.Close()
		close(done)
	}()

	for {
		select {
		case m, ok := <-msgs:
			if !ok {
				// channel closed → shutdown
				f.Sync()
				return
			}
			line := fmt.Sprintf("%s %s\n", m.timestamp.UTC().Format(time.RFC3339Nano), m.text)
			if _, err := f.WriteString(line); err != nil {
				// If logging to file fails, try stderr as last resort
				fmt.Fprintf(os.Stderr, "failed write log: %v\n", err)
			}
			// Ensure durability (configurable if you need throughput)
			if err := f.Sync(); err != nil {
				fmt.Fprintf(os.Stderr, "failed sync log: %v\n", err)
			}
		case <-reopen:
			// Reopen log file (e.g., logrotate)
			f.Sync()
			f.Close()
			f = openFile()
		}
	}
}

func main() {
	dir := flag.String("dir", ".", "directory to watch")
	logPath := flag.String("log", "file-watcher-daemon/events.log", "path to events log")
	flag.Parse()

	absDir, err := filepath.Abs(*dir)
	if err != nil {
		log.Fatalf("get abs dir: %v", err)
	}

	watcher, err := fsnotify.NewWatcher()
	if err != nil {
		log.Fatalf("new watcher: %v", err)
	}
	defer watcher.Close()

	if err := watcher.Add(absDir); err != nil {
		log.Fatalf("watch add: %v", err)
	}

	msgCh := make(chan logMessage, 1024)
	reopenCh := make(chan struct{}, 1)
	loggerDone := make(chan struct{})

	go loggerLoop(*logPath, msgCh, reopenCh, loggerDone)

	sendLog := func(format string, a ...interface{}) {
		select {
		case msgCh <- logMessage{timestamp: time.Now(), text: fmt.Sprintf(format, a...)}:
		default:
			// If the channel is full, drop the event but also try to write an overload notice synchronously
			// to avoid silent loss. We do a non-blocking fallback to stderr.
			fmt.Fprintf(os.Stderr, "log channel full, dropping message: "+format+"\n", a...)
		}
	}

	var wg sync.WaitGroup
	wg.Add(1)
	go func() {
		defer wg.Done()
		for {
			select {
			case ev, ok := <-watcher.Events:
				if !ok {
					return
				}
				sendLog("EVENT op=%s path=%s", ev.Op.String(), ev.Name)
				// If a directory is created inside watched dir, optionally add watch for it:
				if ev.Op&fsnotify.Create == fsnotify.Create {
					// try to add watch for new directory
					info, err := os.Stat(ev.Name)
					if err == nil && info.IsDir() {
						if err := watcher.Add(ev.Name); err == nil {
							sendLog("ADDED_WATCH path=%s", ev.Name)
						} else {
							sendLog("FAILED_ADD_WATCH path=%s err=%v", ev.Name, err)
						}
					}
				}
			case err, ok := <-watcher.Errors:
				if !ok {
					return
				}
				sendLog("WATCHER_ERROR err=%v", err)
			}
		}
	}()

	// Setup signals
	sigC := make(chan os.Signal, 1)
	signal.Notify(sigC, syscall.SIGINT, syscall.SIGTERM, syscall.SIGHUP)

MainLoop:
	for {
		sig := <-sigC
		switch sig {
		case syscall.SIGHUP:
			// Reopen log file
			sendLog("SIGNAL SIGHUP received - reopening log")
			// non-blocking send
			select {
			case reopenCh <- struct{}{}:
			default:
			}
		case syscall.SIGINT, syscall.SIGTERM:
			sendLog("SIGNAL %s received - shutting down", sig.String())
			// clean shutdown
			break MainLoop
		default:
			sendLog("SIGNAL %s received - ignoring", sig.String())
		}
	}

	// initiate shutdown
	watcher.Close()            // causes event goroutine to exit
	wg.Wait()                  // wait for watcher goroutine
	close(msgCh)               // tell logger to exit after flushing
	<-loggerDone               // wait for logger to finish fsync/close
	sendLog("shutdown complete") // note: this won't be written because logger is done; instead rely on earlier flush
}
```

How to build and run
- Install fsnotify: go get github.com/fsnotify/fsnotify (or use modules: the code expects modules).
- Build: GO111MODULE=on go build -o /usr/local/bin/file-watcher-daemon main.go
- Run: /usr/local/bin/file-watcher-daemon -dir /path/to/watch -log /var/log/file-watcher-daemon/events.log

systemd unit
Save as /etc/systemd/system/file-watcher-daemon.service:

[Unit]
Description=File Watcher Daemon
After=network.target

[Service]
Type=simple
ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/file-watcher-daemon -dir /path/to/watch -log /var/log/file-watcher-daemon/events.log
Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=5
StandardOutput=null
StandardError=journal
KillSignal=SIGTERM
LimitNOFILE=65536

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target

Notes and trade-offs
- Durability vs throughput: we call file.Sync() after every line to ensure events are on disk. If you expect very high event rates, you can batch and fsync periodically (e.g., every N events or every second) to increase throughput at the cost of small recent-window loss on crash.
- Log rotation: the example handles SIGHUP by reopening the log. If using logrotate, configure it to send SIGHUP or use copytruncate. Alternatively, use systemd/journald instead of a log file.
- Cross-platform: fsnotify supports Linux, macOS, Windows, but implementations differ (inotify vs kqueue vs ReadDirectoryChanges). Behavior for recursive watching differs — the example only watches a single directory and adds watches for newly created subdirectories but doesn't recursively traverse existing subtrees by default. If you need full recursive watches, add a startup pass to walk subdirs and add watchers.
- If you truly must use raw OS-level signal handlers (C signal handlers) that must log synchronously from the handler itself, we must use the self-pipe trick or signalfd and avoid non-async-safe calls in handlers. The Go pattern used here avoids that and is recommended.

Would you like:
- I can produce a Python version (watchdog) instead, or a C implementation with signalfd/self-pipe if you need strict async-signal-handler logging.
- I can extend the Go program to watch recursively at startup, implement batching, compress/rotate logs, or add an HTTP status endpoint.

Which language and platform do you prefer, and do you want recursive watching or batching for speed?
